WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.700
The latest "Fantastic Beasts" film might just
hold more secrets than Dumbledore himself.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.450
Do you want to learn more about the deluminator,
or clever references to the previous films?

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.264
Keep watching to see the small details you
missed in "Fantastic Beasts: The Secrets of Dumbledore."

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:17.541
What is a wizard to do without a bird to save
the day?

00:00:17.541 --> 00:00:21.260
Go back to 2002 when "Harry Potter and the
Chamber of Secrets" premiered.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.340
Fans had just met Dumbledore's phoenix Fawkes,
who played a significant role in Harry destroying

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.640
Tom Riddle's diary and saving Ginny.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.910
Fawkes carrying Harry, Ron, Ginny, and even
Lockhart out of the Chamber even made it onto

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.090
the cover of the American version of the book.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:38.888
Now, with the events of "The Secrets of Dumbledore,"
phoenixes are more integral to the plot than ever.

00:00:38.888 --> 00:00:42.190
However, it's not a phoenix that offers the
"Chamber of Secrets" Easter egg.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.480
Toward the beginning of the movie, Newt faces
off against Grindelwald's followers.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.899
Mirroring the Fawkes scene from decades later,
a dragon-like creature called a Wyvern flies

00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:56.070
Newt, his suitcase, and his beasts away from
the area as it puffs up like a dragon pufferfish.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.829
It's a significant homage to Fawkes — who
might just be Credence's phoenix.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.699
There's no denying that Sirius Black is one
of the greatest characters to appear in the

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:05.960
"Harry Potter" books and films.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.420
But he's also deeply misunderstood.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.440
Here is a man who's had his reputation obliterated
by a supposed friend and was tortured in Azkaban

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:13.950
for 12 years.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.060
Yet despite his deep trauma and the overwhelming
depression he sometimes faces, Sirius Black

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.800
still manages to be a good person.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.110
"We've all got both light and dark inside
us.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.520
What matters is the part we choose to act
on."

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.180
But between his wisdom and sadness, Sirius
has an unparalleled zest for life and an infectious

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.740
yearning for mischief.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.150
He's everything Grindelwald could be if he
wanted, but the budding dictator chooses to

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.060
kill people instead.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.020
Much of the third "Harry Potter" book and
movie hinges on the manhunt for Sirius, with

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.700
Hogsmeade, Diagon Alley, and Hogwarts littered
with moving Wanted posters.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.641
Now "The Secrets of Dumbledore" is giving
fans deja vu with 

00:01:51.641 --> 00:01:54.400
Grindelwald's snowy Hogsmeade wanted signs.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.329
However, Grindelwald is exonerated for the
crimes he committed, while Sirius must go

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:00.290
into hiding until his death.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.100
Even in the wizarding world, life isn't fair.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.220
Who needs flying cars when you can have magical
trains?

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.759
Some of the "Secrets of Dumbledore" references
to "Harry Potter" are pretty on the nose,

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.330
but it doesn't make them any less charming.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:15.510
While the Wizarding World films often focus
on the beauty of humanity, they also encourage

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.689
fans to appreciate the breathtaking beauty
around them in nature.

00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:20.930
The idea for the Hogwarts Express came to
J.K.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.159
Rowling as she rode on the very train that
inspired her to write "Harry Potter and the

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:25.489
Sorcerer's Stone."

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:29.700
And in its way, the Express gives new students
to Hogwarts a glimpse of magic before they

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.319
even raise their wands for the first time.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:35.510
"The Secrets of Dumbledore" had a similar
idea, when Newt's ragtag group of misfits

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:38.069
meet on a train while it flies through snowy
mountains.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:43.260
It may be a fairly plain-looking train, but
the homage to the Hogwarts Express is palpable.

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:48.120
Dumbledore is known for his sage wisdom, and
even 50 years younger than his first appearance

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.629
in the "Harry Potter" universe, he doesn't
disappoint.

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:54.139
The future headmaster has gone through more
than a few internal struggles and successfully

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:55.900
fought off a lingering thirst for power.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.930
"With or without you, I'll burn down their
world."

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.760
Dumbledore has long since ditched the "for
the greater good" mantra he lived by in his

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.280
time with Grindelwald, but he still occasionally
falls into that mentality.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.250
The most devastating example of this kind
of lapse is his secretive training of Harry

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.999
to prepare him to take down Voldemort.

00:03:11.999 --> 00:03:16.844
He went through with this risky plan knowing
full well that Harry might not survive the conflict.

00:03:16.844 --> 00:03:20.950
Despite his many flaws, Dumbledore gives Harry
some food for thought after Cedric's "Goblet

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.000
of Fire" death.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.150
"Dark and difficult times lie ahead.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:29.129
Soon, we must all face the choice between
what is right and what is easy."

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:33.731
Of course, he's had a long time to perfect
the sentiment between the events of "The Secrets

00:03:33.731 --> 00:03:35.650
of Dumbledore" and "Goblet of Fire."

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:41.040
He gives Newt a similar message to relay to
Vogel, who holds Grindelwald's fate in his hands.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.430
So, what's the message?

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.580
"Do what is right, not what is easy."

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.799
Later, answering the question of why Grindelwald
can run for Supreme Mugwump despite being

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:50.870
a wannabe dictator, Dumbledore says,

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.629
"Because Vogel chose easy over right."

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:57.430
Apparently, Dumbledore was a bit pithier in
his youth, but the sentiment is the same.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:02.260
In "The Deathly Hallows," Grindelwald and
Dumbledore's previous obsession with the fabled

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.889
relics of the Peverell brothers comes to light.

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:08.599
Not only was Grindelwald on the hunt for the
Elder Wand, the Invisibility Cloak, and the

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:12.790
Resurrection Stone, but Dumbledore found himself
on this quest for power as well.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.110
Until this point in the series, Grindelwald's
Hallows obsession goes relatively unmentioned

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:21.019
beyond the reveal that he obtained the Elder
Wand from esteemed wandmaker Gregorovitch.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:25.159
Yet it looks like the Hallows may play a greater
role in Grindelwald's continued quest for

00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:27.060
domination over the muggle world.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.819
In "The Secrets of Dumbledore," fans learn
that each wizard running for Supreme Mugwump

00:04:30.819 --> 00:04:33.180
has a symbol associated with them.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.400
Wizards cast their vote for the next leader
by firing off their nominee's symbol from

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.590
their wand.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.460
In a blink-and-you'll-miss-it scene, it's
clear that the Deathly Hallows symbol inspired

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.680
Grindelwald's mark.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.729
The Hallows symbol lies in the middle of two
Gs, with a more pronounced Elder Wand nestled

00:04:47.729 --> 00:04:49.389
distinctly in the middle.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:53.080
Along with the Hallows reference, the symbol
is eerily similar to the Death Eaters' Dark

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.990
Mark, right down to the lime green color.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.639
Though attacked by his werewolf professor
and discovering that his best friend was sleeping

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:04.090
with a 40-year-old rat, Harry's most traumatic
experience in "The Prisoner of Azkaban" might

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:07.520
be when his Care of Magical Creatures book
tries to eat him.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.900
When the students call out Hagrid for his
bizarre choice of biting books, the giant

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.069
defends himself.

00:05:12.069 --> 00:05:15.530
He calls the books cute and instructs the
students to "stroke the spine" to prevent

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.889
their textbook from attacking.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:22.020
As it turns out, the wizarding world isn't
quite finished with "The Monster Book of Monsters."

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.409
When Dumbledore's army sets off to confuse
Grindelwald and get the Qilin to the election,

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:30.560
all but one group member carries a bewitched
suitcase that looks like Newt's.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.280
Amongst other things, one has a golden snitch,
another has pastries, and yet another melts.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.730
Of course, there's also one with colorful
textbooks.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:44.134
Some of the titles include "Sonnet of a Sorcerer,"
which jinxes readers into speaking in limericks forever.

00:05:44.134 --> 00:05:48.250
Then there's "Have a Fiesta in a Bottle,"
and "Extreme Incantations."

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.409
But alongside the colorful creations is the
dreaded "The Monster Book of Monsters," which

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:54.550
means that Harry's textbook is quite old.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:59.259
It may have even been released before Newt's
book "Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them."

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.759
If there's one thing the "Fantastic Beasts"
series doesn't need, it's romanticizing Severus

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:06.770
Snape's creepy behavior toward Lily Potter.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.080
In "The Deathly Hallows," fans learn about
Snape's obsessive "love" for Lily through

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.080
Snape's memories.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.340
Sure, Snape turned his back on Voldemort,
but only after the dictator killed Lily — Snape

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.510
was fine with her family getting killed, as
long as she survived.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:24.139
Snape's pining over the woman who turned him
down decades ago is endless and ridiculous,

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:28.199
to the point where he bullies her child (just
because Harry looks like James).

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:33.870
When Dumbledore asks if he loves Lily after
all this time, the potions master responds:

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.639
"Always."

00:06:35.639 --> 00:06:40.090
This is stalker behavior, but even Harry himself
romanticizes Snape's actions when he names

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.580
his second son "Albus Severus."

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.530
In "The Secrets of Dumbledore," following
the reveal that Credence is Aberforth's son,

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.500
it's unclear why Aberforth never bothered
to look for him.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.110
When Credence finally meets Aberforth, he
asks, "Did you ever think of me?"

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:56.289
Paying homage to Snape, Aberforth says, "Always."

00:06:56.289 --> 00:07:00.289
Aberforth tells Credence to come home, only
taking responsibility as a father when his

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:02.691
son is dying — and there's nothing that
can save him.

00:07:02.691 --> 00:07:06.940
In some ways, Snape and Aberforth are alike
- and not in good ways.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.010
There's no better title for the third installment
of the "Fantastic Beasts" series than "The

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:13.500
Secrets of Dumbledore."

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.080
It turns out the future headmaster of Hogwarts
is harder to read than a hexed poetry book.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.314
His instinct is to keep everything to himself
until the moment when he needs his allies' help.

00:07:22.314 --> 00:07:27.199
"Dumbledore's got a real sort of checkered
history of being able to encourage people

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:29.259
to do crazy things."

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:32.509
The upshot of this is that he has a wealth
of knowledge about the wizarding world that

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:36.999
he keeps quiet, along with more than a few
wickedly powerful gadgets that he either procured

00:07:36.999 --> 00:07:38.680
or created himself.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.099
One such gadget takes the form of a stopwatch.

00:07:41.099 --> 00:07:43.050
The first "Harry Potter" book describes it
as,

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.629
"A very odd watch.

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:49.500
It had twelve hands but no numbers; instead,
little planets were moving around the edge."

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.310
Dumbledore uses it to tell time, and it makes
an appearance in the first and fifth books.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:57.180
However, it never shows up on screen until
the "Secrets of Dumbledore."

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.360
Here, Dumbledore clearly uses the pocket watch
to find Credence's location, and he has a

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.020
picture of the wizard on the stopwatch.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:07.629
It's no surprise that the mysterious-looking
watch would have a use beyond telling time.

00:08:07.629 --> 00:08:11.279
But it's unclear if it works to track down
just anyone, or if it's a Dumbledore heirloom

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:13.569
specifically made to keep tabs on his bloodline.

00:08:13.569 --> 00:08:17.929
Either way, it's fun to see an aspect of the
book come to life on screen for the first time.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.490
The wizarding world is chock-full of secret
passageways and unique ways that the magical

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.629
community separates their world from the muggles.

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:30.000
For instance, Hogwarts has a protection spell
that causes muggles to see a danger sign atop ruins.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.630
When it comes to the entrance to Diagon Alley,
Hagrid shows Harry how to enter without apparating

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.409
or using floo powder.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:39.930
He simply taps a combination of bricks, and
the wall opens up.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:44.089
We haven't seen this method of entry in any
other magical location since then, but "The

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:48.432
Secrets of Dumbledore" brings it back when
Dumbledore's original army goes to the German Ministry.

00:08:48.432 --> 00:08:52.638
The bricks are just a tad bit sleeker, but
the process is the same.

00:08:52.638 --> 00:08:56.900
Cockroach clusters are peanut-type candy made
of, you guessed it, cockroaches.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:01.290
These bizarre "treats" are purchased more
often to troll unsuspecting Hogwarts classmates

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.160
than to actually eat and enjoy.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:07.139
Ron fails to trick Fred into eating them,
and as it turns out, the Slytherins in "The

00:09:07.139 --> 00:09:08.820
Secrets of Dumbledore" have a similar idea.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:13.130
When Jacob hangs out in the Great Hall, some
"nice" Slytherins offer him candy.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:17.130
After Dumbledore declares that he never cared
much for "Cockroach Clusters," Jacob glares

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.260
at the mischievous Slytherins.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.630
Despite Dumbledore's distaste for cockroach
clusters and earwax Bertie Bott's, he chooses

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.320
"Cockroach Clusters" as a password for his
office about 50 years later.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.540
Maybe he just misses his Muggle friend.

00:09:30.357 --> 00:09:34.709
Movie fans were introduced to the world of
"Harry Potter" when Dumbledore used his deluminator,

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:38.790
a light-sucking magical object of his own
making, to darken Privet Drive.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:42.510
For a while, it seemed like the object's only
use was to suck up and release the light from

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:43.960
various light sources.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.720
Then in "The Deathly Hallows," Ron inherits
the deluminator from Dumbledore.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:51.740
Not only does the object control light sources,
but it seems to be a listening device, too.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:55.910
After Ron bails on Harry and Hermione during
their Horcrux search, he hears snippets of

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:59.740
conversations from the object when Harry and
Hermione mention his name.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:04.010
Even weirder, it acts like a Portkey to apparate
Ron back to his friends when he realizes leaving

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:05.010
was a mistake.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:11.100
"So I took it, clicked it, and this tiny ball
of light appeared."

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:15.399
The full extent of the deluminator's powers
is never fully explored in the original books

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:21.089
and movies, but "The Secrets of Dumbledore"
provides some insight into the device's capabilities.

00:10:21.089 --> 00:10:24.880
Dumbledore uses it in his battle with Credence
and later Grindelwald, where the object does

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.730
something very peculiar.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:31.180
It seems like the deluminator takes both sets
of dueling wizards to a parallel plane adjacent

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:35.050
to the real world, but it looks like a darker
mirrored version.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:38.860
It seems like people on the physical plane
can't see what happens during this duel.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.220
However, one thing's for sure: The deluminator
has quite a few tricks up its sleeve — and

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:44.990
so does Dumbledore.

